---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const projectFacts = [
  ["Project", "Drimini's own website"],
  ["Status", "Live and operated"],
  ["Released", "October 2026"],
  ["Stack", "Astro, TypeScript, Cloudflare Pages, D1"],
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Drimini website case study"
  description="How Drimini framed, built, hardened, and now operates its website and contact system."
  image="/brand/drimini-website-case-study-og.png"
  imageAlt="Case study: building Drimini's first website and contact system."
  ogType="article"
>
  <article class="case-study">
    <header class="case-hero shell">
      <div class="case-hero__copy">
        <p class="eyebrow">Case study / Self-initiated work</p>
        <h1>Building Drimini's first website.</h1>
        <p class="case-hero__lede">
          We needed a credible public front door before we had client logos,
          case studies, or a finished company around it. So we made the constraints
          visible and treated the website as the first piece of proof.
        </p>
      </div>
      <aside class="case-disclosure" aria-label="Project disclosure">
        <strong>This is Drimini's own project.</strong>
        <p>It demonstrates our decisions and implementation, not client outcomes.</p>
      </aside>
    </header>

    <dl class="case-facts shell">
      {projectFacts.map(([term, detail]) => (
        <div>
          <dt>{term}</dt>
          <dd>{detail}</dd>
        </div>
      ))}
    </dl>

    <section class="case-section shell" aria-labelledby="case-constraint-title">
      <div class="case-section__label">
        <span>00</span>
        <p>The constraint</p>
      </div>
      <div class="case-section__content">
        <h2 id="case-constraint-title">Build trust without pretending trust already existed.</h2>
        <p>
          The primary reader is a CTO considering an external team for difficult
          work. At launch, Drimini had no customer results, approved team roster,
          service packages, or finished visual identity to lean on. The site had
          to explain the offer clearly while refusing the usual shortcuts: fake
          scale, decorative AI claims, invented proof, or a contact form that went
          nowhere.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-loop" aria-labelledby="case-loop-title">
      <div class="shell">
        <div class="case-loop__heading">
          <p class="eyebrow">The working loop</p>
          <h2 id="case-loop-title">Frame. Build. Harden.</h2>
        </div>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <span>01 / Frame</span>
            <h3>Make the decision surface small.</h3>
            <p>
              We fixed one reader, one primary action, three capability areas,
              and a direct voice. Static delivery, no analytics, and no CMS kept
              the technical surface proportional to a small launch site.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span>02 / Build</span>
            <h3>Ship the whole path, not a facade.</h3>
            <p>
              Astro produces a JavaScript-free homepage. The contact route uses
              a narrow same-origin function, Turnstile, server-side validation,
              and D1 storage. A local authenticated command handles inbox review;
              there is no public admin surface.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span>03 / Harden</span>
            <h3>Test the risks the site actually has.</h3>
            <p>
              The backend fails closed when storage or spam protection is missing.
              Automated checks cover submission behavior, the inbox lifecycle,
              narrow layouts, page weight, and WCAG 2.2 AA-detectable issues.
              A human submission was exercised from receipt through permanent deletion.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section shell" aria-labelledby="case-decisions-title">
      <div class="case-section__label">
        <span>04</span>
        <p>Decisions left visible</p>
      </div>
      <div class="case-section__content">
        <h2 id="case-decisions-title">Small does not mean vague.</h2>
        <div class="case-decision-grid">
          <div>
            <h3>No client-side application shell</h3>
            <p>The homepage needs content and navigation, so it ships no browser JavaScript.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>No automatic email pipeline</h3>
            <p>Enquiries land in D1. A person reviews them and replies through the existing mailbox.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>No public admin page</h3>
            <p>Inbox access stays behind authenticated Cloudflare tooling instead of creating another attack surface.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>No invented maturity</h3>
            <p>The provisional logo, missing client proof, and remaining company decisions stay labeled as such.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-outcome" aria-labelledby="case-outcome-title">
      <div class="shell case-outcome__grid">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Current outcome</p>
          <h2 id="case-outcome-title">A live, owned starting point.</h2>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p>
            The site is live at drimini.com. Its enquiry path has been verified
            end to end, its operating routine has an owner, and its automated
            behavior and accessibility checks run before release.
          </p>
          <p class="case-outcome__limit">
            What this does not prove: client results, market demand, or how the
            system behaves at a scale it has not reached. Those require future evidence.
          </p>
          <a class="button button--primary" href="/contact">Bring us your difficult bit <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
